1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,830
Between 2019 and 2022, a driver in Ho Chi
Minh City kept a notebook.

2
00:00:04,830 --> 00:00:11,293
According to prosecutors, it recorded cash he carried
out of one bank, on his boss's orders.

3
00:00:11,843 --> 00:00:13,034
The total:

4
00:00:13,034 --> 00:00:18,989
108 trillion dong. More than four billion
dollars. In cash.

5
00:00:19,989 --> 00:00:23,546
At the same bank, tens of thousands of
ordinary savers

6
00:00:23,546 --> 00:00:27,459
were being offered something that sounded
as safe as a deposit.

7
00:00:27,459 --> 00:00:28,098
It wasn't.

8
00:00:28,348 --> 00:00:32,911
This is the story of Truong My Lan. A real
estate tycoon who,

9
00:00:32,911 --> 00:00:38,528
a court found, secretly controlled a major Vietnamese
bank, and used it as her own wallet.

10
00:00:40,028 --> 00:00:44,455
Ho Chi Minh City. Vietnam's biggest city
and its money capital.

11
00:00:44,455 --> 00:00:50,217
In the 1980s and 90s, as Vietnam opened
its economy, fortunes were made here fast.

12
00:00:50,767 --> 00:00:55,802
Truong My Lan was born in Saigon in 1956,
into a family of Chinese descent.

13
00:00:55,802 --> 00:01:01,138
According to state media and international reporting,
she started out selling cosmetics with

14
00:01:01,138 --> 00:01:06,475
her mother at a stall in Ben Thanh Market,
the city's oldest market.

15
00:01:07,025 --> 00:01:10,658
In 1992, she founded a company called Van
Thinh Phat.

16
00:01:10,658 --> 00:01:16,828
Over the next thirty years, it grew into one of the
biggest property groups in the country.

17
00:01:16,828 --> 00:01:21,764
Luxury hotels. Office towers. Shopping
malls in the heart of District 1.

18
00:01:22,314 --> 00:01:26,505
She married a Hong Kong businessman, and
kept a low public profile.

19
00:01:26,505 --> 00:01:31,383
To most Vietnamese, she was simply one
more name in a booming property market.

20
00:01:31,633 --> 00:01:36,161
Her niece would later run the group as its
chief executive. Her husband

21
00:01:36,161 --> 00:01:40,689
stood beside her in business. It was, in
many ways, a family empire.

22
00:01:40,689 --> 00:01:44,078
But building towers takes enormous amounts
of money.

23
00:01:44,078 --> 00:01:47,661
And the cheapest money, if you can get it,
is a bank's.

24
00:01:48,861 --> 00:01:53,558
In late 2011, in a merger approved by the
central bank,

25
00:01:53,558 --> 00:01:58,255
three struggling lenders became one:
Saigon Commercial Bank, known as SCB.

26
00:01:58,255 --> 00:02:00,512
It was supposed to be a rescue.

27
00:02:00,512 --> 00:02:04,080
According to the court, it became
something else.

28
00:02:04,630 --> 00:02:10,121
Vietnamese law limits how much of a bank
any one person can own. On paper,

29
00:02:10,121 --> 00:02:14,880
Lan held a small stake. State media put it
at about five percent.

30
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:18,830
But the Ho Chi Minh City court later found
that she

31
00:02:18,830 --> 00:02:23,139
was the real owner of more than 91.5
percent of SCB's shares.

32
00:02:23,689 --> 00:02:28,484
How do you own nine-tenths of a bank while
appearing to own almost nothing?

33
00:02:28,484 --> 00:02:28,996
Proxies.

34
00:02:29,246 --> 00:02:35,190
According to investigators, relatives, employees and associates
held shares in their own names. The court said

35
00:02:35,190 --> 00:02:41,505
the people holding more than 75 percent of the shares
all admitted they were acting for her.

36
00:02:42,055 --> 00:02:44,462
And with the shares came the people.

37
00:02:44,462 --> 00:02:49,244
The court found that from 2012 she picked
the bank's key managers,

38
00:02:49,244 --> 00:02:54,425
set up special units to handle lending and
payouts, and gave instructions directly.

39
00:02:54,425 --> 00:02:57,033
Officially, she was not on SCB's board.

40
00:02:57,283 --> 00:03:00,658
In practice, the court said, the bank
answered to her.

41
00:03:01,858 --> 00:03:07,951
So how do you move billions of dollars out of a bank you
secretly control, without anyone noticing?

42
00:03:07,951 --> 00:03:11,246
The court laid out the method. Let's walk
through it.

43
00:03:11,796 --> 00:03:13,098
Step one. Companies.

44
00:03:13,098 --> 00:03:18,402
According to state media, investigators counted
more than a thousand companies in the

45
00:03:18,402 --> 00:03:23,707
Van Thinh Phat network. Many of them
existed mainly on paper. Ghost companies,

46
00:03:23,707 --> 00:03:27,743
with people listed as directors who often
didn't run anything.

47
00:03:28,293 --> 00:03:29,570
Step two. Loans.

48
00:03:29,570 --> 00:03:33,911
Those companies applied to SCB for loans.
Fake applications,

49
00:03:33,911 --> 00:03:40,182
the court found, approved by managers who
knew where the money was going.

50
00:03:40,182 --> 00:03:42,646
Between 2018 and 2022 alone,

51
00:03:42,646 --> 00:03:48,561
the court counted 916 such loan applications,
worth around 545 trillion dong.

52
00:03:49,111 --> 00:03:50,669
Step three. Collateral.

53
00:03:50,669 --> 00:03:54,079
Every loan needs security. According to
the court,

54
00:03:54,079 --> 00:04:00,899
appraisal firms colluded to inflate the value of the assets
pledged, so the paperwork looked covered.

55
00:04:01,449 --> 00:04:03,494
Step four. The money moves on.

56
00:04:03,494 --> 00:04:07,033
Once disbursed, the loan money was
shuffled between accounts,

57
00:04:07,033 --> 00:04:13,718
used to pay off older loans, poured into Van Thinh
Phat's projects, or taken out in cash.

58
00:04:13,968 --> 00:04:19,819
Each step, on its own, looked like
ordinary banking. A loan. A valuation. A

59
00:04:19,819 --> 00:04:25,670
transfer. Only together did they form a
pipe that ran in one direction: out.

60
00:04:26,220 --> 00:04:29,340
And here's where the driver comes in.

61
00:04:29,340 --> 00:04:33,330
According to prosecutors, from February
2019 to 2022,

62
00:04:33,330 --> 00:04:40,811
Lan's driver carried cash from SCB to her company's
headquarters and to her home. His

63
00:04:40,811 --> 00:04:46,796
notebook added up to 108 trillion dong,
plus 14.7 million US dollars.

64
00:04:47,346 --> 00:04:52,885
Even in Vietnam's largest banknote, that
is more than two hundred million notes.

65
00:04:53,435 --> 00:05:00,415
Over ten years, the court said, SCB made
more than 2,500 loans into Lan's network.

66
00:05:00,415 --> 00:05:07,054
It found that she had embezzled 304
trillion dong. About 12.5 billion dollars.

67
00:05:07,054 --> 00:05:10,748
International media noted that was close
to

68
00:05:10,748 --> 00:05:14,970
three percent of Vietnam's entire economy
in 2022.

69
00:05:16,170 --> 00:05:19,527
A hole that big should be visible from the
outside.

70
00:05:19,527 --> 00:05:23,938
Banks in Vietnam are inspected by the
State Bank, the central bank.

71
00:05:23,938 --> 00:05:25,386
And SCB was inspected.

72
00:05:25,936 --> 00:05:30,157
According to the court, the bank's general
director paid the head

73
00:05:30,157 --> 00:05:34,378
of a central-bank inspection team 5.2
million dollars, in four payments.

74
00:05:34,378 --> 00:05:40,601
International reporting from the trial described some of
that cash being delivered in styrofoam boxes.

75
00:05:40,851 --> 00:05:43,221
In return, the court found,

76
00:05:43,221 --> 00:05:47,960
the inspection findings were softened.
SCB's real condition stayed hidden.

77
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:54,717
So the bank kept looking healthy on paper. Depositors
kept depositing. And the hole kept growing.

78
00:05:55,267 --> 00:06:00,893
The inspection chief was sentenced to life
in prison. Her appeal was rejected.

79
00:06:00,893 --> 00:06:07,044
She was one of fifteen former central-bank
officials who stood trial alongside Lan,

80
00:06:07,044 --> 00:06:12,721
along with officials from the Government
Inspectorate and the State Audit Office.

81
00:06:13,721 --> 00:06:15,350
Think about what that means.

82
00:06:15,350 --> 00:06:21,748
The people whose job was to check the bank were, according to
the court, part of how the bank stayed unchecked.

83
00:06:22,948 --> 00:06:29,696
From 2018, the court found, Lan's group tapped a
second source of cash: ordinary savers.

84
00:06:30,246 --> 00:06:36,739
Four companies linked to Van Thinh Phat issued 25 batches
of corporate bonds between 2018 and 2020.

85
00:06:36,739 --> 00:06:43,233
The court found these were fake bonds, and the money
raised was never used for the purposes stated.

86
00:06:43,483 --> 00:06:47,187
They were sold through a securities firm
and SCB's own branches.

87
00:06:47,437 --> 00:06:53,393
To a saver, the difference between a deposit and a
bond can look like a few lines of

88
00:06:53,393 --> 00:06:58,356
small print. A deposit is a claim on the
bank, backed, up to a limit,

89
00:06:58,356 --> 00:07:05,304
by deposit insurance. A corporate bond is a loan to a
company. If the company can't pay, you can lose it.

90
00:07:05,854 --> 00:07:09,880
Most of the buyers were SCB depositors.
According to state media,

91
00:07:09,880 --> 00:07:14,272
bank staff reassured them the bonds were
safe, like a savings account.

92
00:07:14,522 --> 00:07:21,431
State media also reported that the bank's general director held training
sessions for thousands of sales staff on how to sell them.

93
00:07:21,981 --> 00:07:26,004
The court counted 35,824 victims and more
than

94
00:07:26,004 --> 00:07:30,026
30 trillion dong taken, about 1.2 billion
dollars.

95
00:07:30,026 --> 00:07:36,647
Where did it go? Repaying bank loans.
Paying interest on older bonds. Projects.

96
00:07:36,647 --> 00:07:41,927
Transfers abroad. And, the court said,
Lan's personal spending.

97
00:07:43,127 --> 00:07:45,937
In October 2022, it all came apart.

98
00:07:45,937 --> 00:07:48,346
Police arrested Truong My Lan.

99
00:07:48,896 --> 00:07:54,101
Within days, worried depositors crowded
SCB branches to pull out their savings.

100
00:07:54,101 --> 00:08:00,756
For many families, SCB was simply the bank down the street.
They had no idea who really controlled it.

101
00:08:01,006 --> 00:08:07,831
The State Bank placed SCB under special control and sent in
managers from the big state-owned banks.

102
00:08:08,381 --> 00:08:12,347
To keep the bank alive, the central bank
kept lending to it.

103
00:08:12,347 --> 00:08:18,428
By February 2025, Reuters reported, the central bank
had lent SCB about 25.6 billion dollars.

104
00:08:18,428 --> 00:08:23,848
And the bondholders discovered that the companies
behind their bonds could not pay.

105
00:08:24,098 --> 00:08:27,814
According to state media, they filed
complaints with police

106
00:08:27,814 --> 00:08:31,529
and petitioned the authorities, asking for
their money back.

107
00:08:31,529 --> 00:08:35,543
In effect, central-bank money was keeping
a private bank alive,

108
00:08:35,543 --> 00:08:39,959
because letting it fail risked spreading
panic through the whole system.

109
00:08:41,159 --> 00:08:46,065
On March 5, 2024, the trial opened at the
Ho Chi Minh City People's Court.

110
00:08:46,065 --> 00:08:50,750
It was part of the Communist Party's
long-running anti-corruption campaign,

111
00:08:50,750 --> 00:08:53,093
known as the "blazing furnace".

112
00:08:53,643 --> 00:09:00,606
Eighty-six defendants. About 2,700 people
summoned as witnesses. Around 200 lawyers.

113
00:09:00,606 --> 00:09:06,492
And, according to the BBC, 104 boxes of
case files weighing six tonnes.

114
00:09:06,492 --> 00:09:10,595
Lan denied embezzling. She said she had
only a

115
00:09:10,595 --> 00:09:14,699
small stake in the bank, and blamed her
subordinates.

116
00:09:14,949 --> 00:09:20,176
The court rejected that. It said she had orchestrated
a sophisticated criminal enterprise.

117
00:09:20,726 --> 00:09:24,343
On April 11, 2024, the court gave its
verdict.

118
00:09:24,893 --> 00:09:31,148
Truong My Lan: guilty of embezzlement,
bribery, and violating banking rules.

119
00:09:31,148 --> 00:09:32,795
The sentence: death.

120
00:09:32,795 --> 00:09:37,695
The court ordered her to pay back around
674 trillion dong,

121
00:09:37,695 --> 00:09:40,367
about 27 billion dollars, to SCB.

122
00:09:40,367 --> 00:09:45,717
It said there was little chance the money
could all be recovered.

123
00:09:46,267 --> 00:09:51,680
Her husband got nine years. Her niece, who
ran Van Thinh Phat, got seventeen.

124
00:09:51,680 --> 00:09:54,472
Four defendants were sentenced to life:

125
00:09:54,472 --> 00:10:01,453
two former chairmen of the bank, its former general
director, and the central-bank inspection chief.

126
00:10:01,453 --> 00:10:04,054
Every one of the 86 was found guilty.

127
00:10:05,254 --> 00:10:11,405
In the autumn of 2024, Lan was back in the
same court, this time for the bonds.

128
00:10:11,405 --> 00:10:15,733
On October 17, she was found guilty of
fraud,

129
00:10:15,733 --> 00:10:19,580
money laundering, and illegally moving
money across borders.

130
00:10:19,830 --> 00:10:25,447
The court found she had laundered more
than 445 trillion dong,

131
00:10:25,447 --> 00:10:33,617
and illegally moved more than 4.5 billion dollars across
Vietnam's borders. She was sentenced to life.

132
00:10:34,167 --> 00:10:38,474
In December 2024, an appeals court upheld
her death sentence.

133
00:10:38,474 --> 00:10:42,852
But under Vietnamese law, it noted, she
could avoid execution if

134
00:10:42,852 --> 00:10:47,229
she paid back at least three-quarters of
what she had taken.

135
00:10:47,479 --> 00:10:50,432
International media called it a race
against time:

136
00:10:50,432 --> 00:10:55,231
her life seemed to depend on how fast her
empire could be sold.

137
00:10:55,481 --> 00:10:59,589
In April 2025, ruling on her appeal in the
bond case,

138
00:10:59,589 --> 00:11:07,059
the court cut her fraud sentence from life to twenty years, after
money began to be recovered for the bondholders.

139
00:11:08,059 --> 00:11:10,091
Then the law itself changed.

140
00:11:10,091 --> 00:11:11,688
In June 2025,

141
00:11:11,688 --> 00:11:18,074
Vietnam's National Assembly abolished the death penalty
for eight crimes, including embezzlement.

142
00:11:18,324 --> 00:11:23,248
Death sentences already handed down for
those crimes would not be carried out.

143
00:11:23,248 --> 00:11:28,550
Instead, the head of the Supreme People's Court
would convert them to life in prison.

144
00:11:28,550 --> 00:11:32,022
Her lawyer told AFP that Truong My Lan
would be spared.

145
00:11:33,222 --> 00:11:37,730
For the victims, the question was never
her sentence. It was their money.

146
00:11:38,280 --> 00:11:42,479
Investigators had frozen more than 1,200
properties linked to her,

147
00:11:42,479 --> 00:11:48,777
and hundreds of millions of SCB shares
held in her name or her proxies' names.

148
00:11:48,777 --> 00:11:55,337
Ho Chi Minh City's civil judgment enforcement office
began paying bondholders in June 2025.

149
00:11:55,587 --> 00:12:02,566
By May 2026, according to the Vietnamese government's
news portal, nine rounds had paid out

150
00:12:02,566 --> 00:12:10,011
about 12 trillion dong, roughly 40 percent of what was
owed, to more than 42,000 creditors.

151
00:12:10,261 --> 00:12:16,245
A tenth payment followed in July 2026. It was worth
about one percent of what was owed.

152
00:12:16,245 --> 00:12:21,464
For a retiree who put their savings into a
bond they believed was a deposit,

153
00:12:21,464 --> 00:12:25,639
that means waiting, year after year, for a
slice at a time.

154
00:12:26,189 --> 00:12:31,412
Her possessions are being sold. Two designer
handbags. A Maybach. A yacht that,

155
00:12:31,412 --> 00:12:34,225
after five auctions, still had no buyer.

156
00:12:34,225 --> 00:12:39,087
She had asked the court to let her family
keep the handbags. It refused.

157
00:12:39,637 --> 00:12:42,886
But the bondholders are only part of the
bill.

158
00:12:42,886 --> 00:12:47,266
The 27 billion dollars owed to SCB is on
another scale entirely.

159
00:12:47,266 --> 00:12:51,646
The court itself warned that much of it
may never come back.

160
00:12:51,896 --> 00:12:55,047
And SCB? As of August 2026,

161
00:12:55,047 --> 00:13:01,349
state media reported, it was still under
special control, and closing branches.

162
00:13:02,549 --> 00:13:05,617
Strip away the numbers, and the shape is
simple.

163
00:13:05,617 --> 00:13:09,580
One. The lender and the borrower are
secretly the same person.

164
00:13:09,580 --> 00:13:13,671
Two. The loans hide behind companies that
exist mostly on paper.

165
00:13:13,671 --> 00:13:17,698
Three. The collateral is inflated, so
everything looks covered.

166
00:13:17,948 --> 00:13:22,310
Four. The people who are supposed to
check, don't. Or are paid not to.

167
00:13:22,310 --> 00:13:26,976
And five. When the hole grows, it gets
filled with money from ordinary savers,

168
00:13:26,976 --> 00:13:29,975
sold something risky as if it were a
deposit.

169
00:13:30,975 --> 00:13:36,942
In this case, one detail made it worse.
The bonds were sold inside the bank,

170
00:13:36,942 --> 00:13:43,307
by people wearing the bank's uniform. The trust
people had in the bank was the product.

171
00:13:43,307 --> 00:13:49,549
This is not unique to Vietnam. Insider lending has
broken banks all over the world.

172
00:13:49,799 --> 00:13:53,528
If someone at a bank counter offers you a
product with a better

173
00:13:53,528 --> 00:13:57,257
rate than a deposit, ask one question: who
is actually borrowing my money?

174
00:13:58,457 --> 00:14:05,121
She began at a market stall. She ended with a court bill
bigger than most companies will ever see.

175
00:14:05,121 --> 00:14:10,960
Her empire was built on property. But what
it really ran on was other people's trust.

176
00:14:10,960 --> 00:14:14,326
And somewhere in between, a bank became a
wallet.

177
00:14:15,326 --> 00:14:19,545
Next time: China. An online platform that
promised safety,

178
00:14:19,545 --> 00:14:25,170
backed by contracts and celebrity ads… and
900,000 investors who believed it.

179
00:14:25,720 --> 00:14:27,340
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

180
00:14:27,340 --> 00:14:32,776
And tell me in the comments: would you know if your bank was
selling you something that wasn't a deposit?

